+# P0 #2053 RCA — supremeai-core nightly CI red: PyErrorFix autofix job vs private-repo code scanning
+
+Repo: `Supremeai-bd/supremeai-core` (private, Layer 2). Incident: workflow `ci` failure on `main`, Run [40653481018](https://github.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-core/actions/runs/40653481018) (schedule, run #40, 2026-10-28T07:58Z). Filed automatically as federation mirror #2053 (P0-critical, handoff:coder).
+
+## Verdict (one line)
+
+NOT a product defect. The `autofix` job's `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3` step is structurally guaranteed to fail on a private repository without Advanced Security — every nightly `schedule` run turns red on that single unprotected step, which then pages the federation watchdog and mirrors a fresh P0 into `supremeai-open` each night.
+
+## Evidence chain (zero-credential, via MCP Tower GitHub bridge)
+
+1. **Workflow source** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, read via `github_read_file`, sha `b69ba39`):
+   - Job `test` (🧪 Core Pytest & Lint) runs on `push`/`pull_request`.
+   - Job `autofix` (🔧 PyErrorFix Engine) runs ONLY on `schedule` (cron `0 2 * * *`) or `workflow_dispatch` with `run_autofix=true`.
+   - Inside the autofix job, BOTH pyerrorfix analyze invocations (the SARIF one and the console one) are guarded with a trailing or-true shell guard, and the SARIF artifact has an empty-document fallback (an unsized or unparseable file gets rewritten as a valid empty SARIF). **None of those can fail the job.**
+   - The ONLY step in `autofix` without failure protection is `Upload SARIF to GitHub code scanning` (`if: always()`, `uses: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3`, no `continue-on-error`).
+2. **Repo visibility** (`github_repo_info`): description self-declares `[PRIVATE] SupremeAI Core — proprietary intelligence ... The moat.` Code scanning upload requires Advanced Security; on a private repo without GHAS, `upload-sarif` fails with an advanced-security-not-enabled class error. Structural, not flaky.
+3. **Run history** (`github_workflow_runs`, 15 newest): push runs 39/38/37 (green), 35 (green) interleaved with schedule runs 40/36/26 — **every `schedule` run failed; every post-fix `push` run passed.** The only red push streak (34–27, 2026-10-26 evening) was a separate fix sequence that has since converged green.
+4. **Failed-job identity across incidents** (`github_get_failed_logs` for runs 40653481018 / 40466830817 / 40286008931): all three failed on the SAME single job `🔧 PyErrorFix Engine`, and all three log tails end in the same post-phase signature (`Cleaning up orphan processes` + the `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3` Node-deprecation warning) — i.e. the run reaches and dies at/around the upload step, never in analyze.
+
+Conclusion: job redness is 100% attributable to the upload step's environment mismatch, recurring nightly by cron, each occurrence re-mirroring a P0 into the open board (watchdog `federation_ci_failed` dispatch in the same workflow guarantees it).
+
+## Proposed fix (admin lane — `.github/` is a protected path, `#1643` precedent; the bridge routes it to admin review, it never auto-lands)
+
+One-line condition on the upload step in `supremeai-core:.github/workflows/ci.yml`:
+
+```diff
+       - name: Upload SARIF to GitHub code scanning
+-        if: always()
++        if: always() && github.event.repository.private != true
+         uses: github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v3
+         with:
+           sarif_file: pyerrorfix-results.sarif
+           category: pyerrorfix
+```
+
+- Private repo (today): step skips, `autofix` goes green, the PyErrorFix detect/analyze/fix logic keeps running exactly as before. Nightly P0 mirror storm stops.
+- Public repo or a future GHAS-enabled private repo: step runs normally — zero capability loss.
+- The analyze steps remain `|| true` (report-only semantics preserved); the SARIF empty-fallback remains; nothing else in the job changes.
+
+## Alternatives considered and rejected
+
+- `continue-on-error: true` on the upload step: keeps attempting a structurally-impossible upload every night, leaves a permanent warning banner, and would also swallow a REAL upload regression if GHAS is ever enabled. The conditional skip is strictly more honest.
+- Delete the upload step outright: loses the working path for public-repo futures and diverges from the original R-intent that wired code scanning in.
+- Move the nightly scan out of `ci.yml` into a separate workflow: bigger diff, same protected-path constraint, no additional benefit.
+- Fork-PR the fix: requires credentials the zero-credential lane does not hold; bridge admin-review is the sanctioned channel.
+
+## Post-fix verification recipe (for the admin lane)
+
+1. Land the one-line diff in `supremeai-core` `main`.
+2. `workflow_dispatch` the `ci` workflow with `run_autofix=true` → expect `autofix` job green with upload skipped (private-repo expression false).
+3. Next nightly cron (02:00 UTC) → expect the run green; the `federation_ci_failed` watchdog step only fires `if: failure()`, so no new mirror is created.
+4. Close mirrors: #2053 (this chain) and any earlier un-closed copies of the same nightly-red class.
